[#:seon.task{:name "references-resolves-each-name-with-its-own-pulls"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`seon.ref/references` over `docs/seon/schedule/agent-loop-ledger.md` (262 KB, 1193 distinct candidates) on default (2026-10-08, pid 9276, warm, `seon.profile/measure`): 102 ms and 125 MB allocated, of which parsing and candidates are 28 ms and 30 MB (a cache hit plus one edamame read per span). The rest is 1372 `seon.db/pull` calls from `seon.ref/resolve-name`, one function pull then one test pull per name, each about 69 KB allocated through the read-evidence and declaration path. Propose: resolve all names of one call in one Datalog query over `:seon.fn/sym` and `:seon.test/sym` with the names as a collection binding, so the read cost follows the number of names once instead of per name. Done when the ledger measurement allocates under 40 MB with the same answers, as a regression on allocation."
             :must-read #{seon.ref/references seon.db/pull}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-doc-section-test-takes-eighteen-seconds"
             :kind :function
             :intent "seon.ref-test/a-doc-section-reads-with-its-headings-below-and-size took 18,404 ms against its 5,000 ms bound on default 795a770c2 (fanout-2 run, 2026-10-08) and errs on every run, so any request reaching seon.ref-test holds a live thread for it past the step's time limit (see `seon.test.runner/a-timed-out-unrelated-test-restarts-in-every-eval-request`). Done when it passes within its bound, with the measured cost of the (my.fs/section \"AGENTS.md#data-and-contracts\") read it runs through a running agent."
             :must-read #{my.fs/section seon.test-support/with-running-agent}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-task-target-reads-as-an-unresolved-reference"
             :kind :function
             :intent "A typed task names the function the agent must create in backticks (`comp.geo/area` in `seon.competency/area-is-contracted-typed`, `:comp.order/qty` in `seon.competency/quantity-schema-then-function-typed`), and `seon.ref/references` answers it as `:problem-kind :unresolved`, the same problem a broken reference gets: the block tells the agent its task is defective exactly where the task is correct. Done when a reference the task's intent names as its target (a name in the task's own subject namespace, or one the intent marks as to be defined) reads as a target to create, distinct from an unresolved reference."
             :must-read #{seon.ref/references seon.context/current-state}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-span-with-words-after-a-name-reads-as-that-name"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`seon.ref/references` over a text whose code spans hold foo/bar baz and seon.ref/section AGENTS.md (each a name followed by more words) answers two `:unresolved` names, foo/bar and seon.ref/section: `seon.ref/span-candidate` reads a code span with `edamame.core/parse-string`, which returns the span's first form and ignores the rest, so prose that merely starts with a qualified name becomes a reference (probe at default 0f0e77ed8, 2026-10-09). A span is a reference only when it reads as exactly one qualified symbol or keyword (AGENTS.md, Documentation is data). Change `seon.ref/span-candidate` to read all forms (`edamame.core/parse-string-all`) and answer a name only for a span of one form. Checked by `seon.ref-test/a-span-of-a-name-and-more-words-is-prose`, which calls `seon.ref/references` directly."
             :must-read #{seon.ref/references seon.ref/span-candidate edamame.core/parse-string edamame.core/parse-string-all}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-missing-anchor-ranks-headings-by-shared-prefix"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`(seon.ref/section \"AGENTS.md#Data-and-contracts\")` answers a `:heading` error whose `:seon.ref/nearest` starts with `seon--shared-instructions`, `the-perspective-an-agent-at-its-repl`, `how-to-think` (document order), not `data-and-contracts`: `seon.ref/section` sorts headings by `seon.ref/common-prefix` with the asked anchor, so a capital letter or a first-letter typo (`#bata-and-contracts`) shares no prefix with anything (probe at default 0f0e77ed8, 2026-10-09). Rank by spelling distance with Malli's `malli.error/levenshtein` (which also orders `data-and-contracts` first for both probes) and delete `seon.ref/common-prefix`. Checked by `seon.ref-test/a-missing-anchor-names-the-closest-spelled-heading-first`, which calls `seon.ref/section` directly."
             :must-read #{seon.ref/section seon.ref/common-prefix malli.error/levenshtein}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-missing-section-reply-shows-only-its-message"
             :kind :function
             :intent "An agent's `(my.fs/section \"AGENTS.md#Data-and-contracts\")` replies with only \"No heading with this anchor.\" (agent tw-probe, default 0f0e77ed8, 2026-10-09): `seon.ref/section-error` puts the nearest headings and the path under `:seon.ref/nearest` and `:seon.ref/path`, and `seon.error/reply-value` (the error's AI face) selects only members in the seon.error namespace, so the docstring's promise, an error naming the nearest headings, never reaches the agent. Fix at the producer: `seon.ref/section-error` also states what was refused and what exists as error members `seon.error/reply-value` already shows (:seon.error/offending the asked path and anchor, :seon.error/expected the nearest), leaving its own seon.ref keys as they are. Checked by `seon.ref-test/a-missing-heading-reply-names-the-nearest-heading`, which calls `seon.error/reply-value` on `seon.ref/section`'s answer."
             :must-read #{seon.ref/section seon.ref/section-error seon.error/reply-value}}
]
